## CONTEXT & OBJECTIVE

You are building a complete, production-ready Python CLI application called **RepoAgent** — an LLM-powered framework that automatically generates, maintains, and updates Markdown documentation for entire Python code repositories. The system was originally published by OpenBMB (https://github.com/OpenBMB/RepoAgent) and presented at EMNLP 2024.

Your job is to implement **every component from scratch**, end-to-end, with no placeholders and no stub functions. Every function must be fully implemented. All files must be wired together and runnable.

---

## HIGH-LEVEL ARCHITECTURE

The system has **three stages**:

1. **Global Structure Analysis** — Parse every Python file in a target repository using Python's `ast` module to build a "Project Tree" (a hierarchical data structure of all code objects: modules, classes, methods, functions). Identify bi-directional call/reference relationships between objects. Store the result as a JSON file (`.project_doc_record`).

2. **Documentation Generation** — Walk the Project Tree in bottom-up topological order (leaves first, following a DAG of reference relationships). For each code object, call an LLM (OpenAI-compatible API) with a structured prompt that includes: the global project tree, the object's raw source code snippet, its reference relationships (callers/callees), its meta-information (type, name, file path), and optionally, already-generated documentation of its direct child nodes. Write output as `.md` files into a configurable `markdown_docs/` folder.

3. **Documentation Update** — Use `gitpython` to diff the target repository against its HEAD commit. Detect which Python files and objects have been added, modified, or deleted. Re-generate or delete only the affected documentation nodes. This runs automatically via a `pre-commit` hook.

There is also a **Chat With Repo** mode (optional fourth module) that enables Q&A over the generated documentation using an embedding-based RAG pipeline.

